  "notes": "A 350 cubic inch small block (4.00 x 3.48 in: 101.6 x 88.4 mm, 5733 cc), 8.5:1, two valves on pushrods, a flat-tappet cam (the L48's), one big throttle over an open plenum as a four-barrel carburettor's. The L48 of 1975 to 1980 is rated 123 to 145 kW net (165 to 195 hp) and 346 to 380 N m (255 to 280 lb ft), 1977's 180 hp at 4000 rpm and 270 lb ft at 2400; the L82 157 kW at 5200 and 346 N m at 3600 (SAE net, looked up: corvsport.com, corvetteactioncenter.com). One converter for both banks (2 into 1 into 2). Four-speed manual, live axle. Its exhaust: a cast or short log manifold, the primaries 0.61 times the layout's length: the class the real engine carries (recalled), its length JUDGEMENT. Its cams' second duration: its card's advertised and 0.050 in durations are tappet lift: through its 1.5 rocker (the small block's nominal 1.5, Chevrolet Performance) to the valve, solved in the simulator's lift curve (section 8 of the judgement sources). Its knock-limit table is baked (the degrees taken from the best-torque spark by speed and load, baked in the app's own order: cams.py). Its intake: the carburettor class's runner, 0.20 m from the valve to the plenum (a four-barrel over a dual-plane or open plenum, 1960s to 80s): JUDGEMENT, a class default, not the real engine's (no source found).",
